  "content": {
    "body": "**Oathkeeper** is Pebbles' chat UI. It is NOT a harness — it sits ON TOP of\none (Claude Code `claude`, Codex `codex`, OpenCode `opencode`; possibly\nOathkeeper itself in the future) and its job is to WIRE and RELAY full\ncontext so the AI gets everything it needs. Every message is wrapped with\nthe platform contract and sent to the harness running inside the project.\n\n## The CONTEXT switch — the single most important behavior\n\nAttaching a context pebble flips the whole mode of a session:\n\n- **WITH context** — you are working ON that specific PERMANENT pebble\n  (model / structure / workflow / finding …). The AI MAY create / update /\n  set-status it and change the code it specifies. Oathkeeper relays the\n  pebble PLUS its **references, sub-references and resources**, so the AI\n  sees the full graph around it. The session's scratch tabs also ride along\n  as context — anything drafted there (a checklist, a workflow) can be\n  FOLDED INTO the pebble.\n- **NO context (scratch session)** — you are working on Oathkeeper's\n  TEMPORARY tabs only. Attached pebbles are **reference-only**: the AI reads\n  them to understand the spec and flows but MUST NOT create / update /\n  set-status / add-finding / link-code on ANY permanent `.pebbles/` record.\n  Investigate, fix code, draft freely — the permanent spec stays untouched;\n  results live in the tabs. To make a permanent change, the user attaches\n  that pebble as context first.\n\nSo: with context = operate on the permanent pebble (+ its whole graph);\nwithout context = operate on the temporary tabs, permanent spec is\nread-only.\n\n## The session tabs (temporary workspace)\n\nAn Oathkeeper session has four sub-tabs — **Chat, Checklist, Workflows,\nFindings**. They are the session's OWN scratch working docs: temporary,\nstored in the app's global chat store (NOT the repo), **uncommitted and\nsafe to delete** — deleting a session never affects permanent spec. They\nmay REFERENCE permanent records (workflow / model / structure / finding) so\nthe AI knows the app's specification and flows, but the work modifies the\ntabs, not those records (unless a context pebble is attached — see the\nswitch). They are for working on things — bug fixes, investigations,\ndrafts.\n\n## What Oathkeeper relays to the AI\n\nEverything the AI needs, wired automatically:\n\n- **The context pebble** — full JSON + file path, and the selected unit\n  (field / item / node) when one is picked.\n- **References + sub-references** — the context's refs resolved (depth-1),\n  AND each ref's own refs (depth-2), deduped and capped.\n- **Resources** — the context's codeLinks resolved from the code MAP, so\n  the AI starts oriented at the real implementation.\n- **Agent / skill wiring** — selecting an agent overlays its persona and\n  resolves its skills, rules and knowledge; invoking a skill carries its\n  procedure. Their referenced rules / knowledge come along.\n- **Attachments** — files (in-project ones annotated with a map hint;\n  `.pebble` files flagged read-only) and folders (explore semantics — other\n  projects for cross-repo context, or skill folders).\n- **Intent** — the chat's general goal (discover / extract / verify / fix /\n  plan / impact / investigate), which also pulls its activity rules.\n- **The session tabs** — the scratch Checklist / Workflows / Findings\n  digest, so the AI sees the workspace it is (or is not) allowed to modify.\n- **The CLI contract + code map status + spec rules + conversation\n  history**, plus the OATH MANIFEST listing exactly what was packed in — if\n  something isn't listed, it isn't in context (query/get it, never guess).\n\n## Harnesses (integration is the point)\n\nOathkeeper's value is TOP-NOTCH harness integration — it should meet each\nharness where it is (including IDE integrations like VS Code). Today Claude\nCode is the most stable; Codex and OpenCode currently have a KNOWN issue\nwith multiple messages in one session (to be fixed). A skill saved for one\nharness works when chatting with any of them.\n\n## Permission enforcement (Oathkeeper is the approver, not the harness)\n\n- Direct Edit / Write on any `.pebbles/` path is DENIED in every mode\n  (bypass included) — spec changes go through `pebbles-cli`. Reads are free.\n- Pure `pebbles-cli` Bash calls are AUTO-ALLOWED; anything else (other\n  programs, `&&`/`|` chaining, redirects, `$(…)`, hijack env names) falls\n  through to a manual prompt.\n- The session permission mode (default / plan / acceptEdits /\n  bypassPermissions) is enforced by Oathkeeper's own approver, so it holds\n  even when org policy disables CLI-level bypass. Denied rules can be\n  granted per-session (never global).\n\n## Run lifecycle\n\nEvery run is journaled (crash recovery kills orphaned processes on next\nlaunch and marks the session resumable) and renders as an APPEND-ONLY\ntimeline — text, terminal-boxed tool calls with captured output, thinking\nmarkers, permission decisions. The same events persist on the saved\nmessage. Chats are per-developer data in the OS app-data dir (never the\nrepo), keyed per project; each session owns its settings (model / effort /\nmode), and a settings change becomes the per-harness default for NEW\nsessions without rewriting existing ones.",
    "kind": "text"
  },
